WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.629
The cold war was a hotbed of innovation and
technological development that gave us incredible

00:00:05.629 --> 00:00:10.980
planes like the SR-71 and Fairchild Republic
A-10 Warthog.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:16.270
But it also gave us some of the worst planes
to ever get past the drawing board.

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:21.290
The cold war gave rise to several bizarre
aircraft like Avro Canada’s take on the

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:26.929
modern day UFO, Northrop’s TACIT Blue stealth
aircraft, which looked like it was designed

00:00:26.929 --> 00:00:28.210
by a 3-year-old child,

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:34.020
or the peculiar Thunderceptor with its booster
rockets motors which was obsolete before it

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.810
even hit the runway.

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:43.010
But perhaps the worst of all, was the relatively
‘normal-looking’ XF-84 Thunderscreech.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:46.320
While it looked normal, it did not sound normal.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.650
The Thunderscreech was so loud that it often
made its ground crew physically ill.

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:56.820
So loud that the power of the sound waves
emitting from the plane were capable of knocking

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:03.840
people over, and on one occasion caused an
engineer working on the plane to suffer a

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:04.840
seizure.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:09.700
This incredible noise this plane created is
what gave the thunderscreech its infamous

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:15.289
name and to this day, holds the world record
for the loudest aircraft ever made.

00:01:15.289 --> 00:01:19.850
[1] Today, we are going to learn why this
abomination was created.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:24.409
Sharing what the plane sounded like here is
practically impossible.

00:01:24.409 --> 00:01:33.560
Recordings simply give us this fairly standard
sounding droning noise.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:45.740
It’s impossible to emulate over digital
audio because the Thunderscreech ungodly noise

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:50.700
was caused by shock waves being thrown outwards
by the strange propeller design.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:56.400
The Thunderscreech was a unique plane, born
out of a unique need during the early development

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.530
years of the jet engine.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:03.979
The navy needed aircraft with short take off
runs so that they could take off from aircraft

00:02:03.979 --> 00:02:08.750
carrier runways to give them a competitive
advantage near enemy territory.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:14.390
This required something called climb performance,
which jets at the time were not particularly

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:17.310
good at, the propeller planes were superior.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:23.750
This means the propeller design allows these
aircraft to take off from aircraft carriers

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:27.150
with much shorter runways and climb faster.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:33.240
However when competing with other more powerful
jet aircraft, speed was the obvious limiting

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.240
factor.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:39.570
That higher maximum power allowed jet engines
to go fast, really fast, and when building

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:43.360
an interceptor aircraft speed is important.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:49.110
Republic designed this abomination in an attempt
to get the best of both worlds.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:51.730
Great climb rate, and great top speed.

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:55.000
The Thunderscreech was a supersonic propeller
design.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.470
A propeller designed to spin faster and achieve
much higher top speeds than a conventional

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:01.470
propeller.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:06.160
Creating a propeller capable of providing
the thrust needed at these higher speeds is

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:07.470
not easy.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:12.940
The velocity of air around a propeller blade
travelling at constant rotational speed increases

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:16.410
steadily from the root of the blade to the
tip.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:21.400
At the tip of the blade, air will be leaving
the propeller disc at a much faster speed

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.150
than at the root of the blade.

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:28.270
Even though the rotational velocity is the
same for all parts of the propellor, the tip

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:33.750
of the blade travels a much further distance
than a point near the root of the propellor,

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:38.220
and therefore, the linear velocity is much
higher at the tip.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:42.910
As the propeller tips approach the speed of
sound, air becomes highly compressible, and

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:48.950
large pressure differences result in bubbles
of unstable air and shock waves around the

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:50.290
propeller tips.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:55.960
The disturbed air results in an increase in
drag and a reduction in propeller efficiency

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:59.720
and could lead to other complications like
propeller stall.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:05.450
Keeping the propeller efficiency as high as
possible at the expected operating speeds

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:10.250
is thus an important criterion in propeller
design.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:15.200
The propeller efficiency can also be thought
of as the ratio of power in the form of thrust

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:20.060
produced by the propeller to the power actually
produced by the engine.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:26.280
This graph shows the typical propeller efficiency
profile of a fixed-pitch WWII aircraft relative

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:27.280
to speed.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:32.449
[2] We can see that the propeller efficiency
rapidly plummets at higher speeds as air becomes

00:04:32.449 --> 00:04:35.150
more unstable over the propeller.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:40.479
Propellers spinning at supersonic speeds,
with this kind of efficiency curve, are untenable.

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:45.770
If propeller planes were to compete with jets,
engineers need a way to prevent this drop

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.530
often in efficiency.

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:52.280
For this reason, the supersonic propeller
of the Thunderscreech was much shorter in

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:53.280
length.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:58.460
Effectively delaying the shock wave formation
for as long as possible, by reducing the circumference

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:02.370
the tip needs to travel during a single rotation.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:07.750
This reduces the tip velocity, a propeller
with half the radius will have half the tip

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:11.189
velocity, even if it is spinning at the same
angular velocity.

00:05:11.189 --> 00:05:15.189
(tip velocity = radius x angular velocity)
However, this alone is not enough to prevent

00:05:15.189 --> 00:05:18.099
the efficiency issues of a supersonic propeller.

00:05:18.099 --> 00:05:24.180
The propeller of the thunderscreech was rotating
at supersonic speeds even when it was on the

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:25.180
runway.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:30.069
The next big difference between conventional
propellers and supersonic propellers is the

00:05:30.069 --> 00:05:36.050
angle at which the blade is placed relative
to the air striking it . Supersonic propellers

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:41.259
are fitted with a much larger angle to the
axis of rotation relative to conventional

00:05:41.259 --> 00:05:46.870
propellers, and critically the thunderscreech
had a variable pitch propeller, meaning the

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:51.849
propeller can change it’s angle of attack
at different speeds to optimize efficiency.

00:05:51.849 --> 00:05:56.189
To understand this, let’s look at a normal
aerofoil cross section.

00:05:56.189 --> 00:06:00.610
When it travels forward, it will provide lift
perpendicular to the chord line.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:05.400
If the aerofoil increases it’s angle of
attack, which is the angle between the direction

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:11.659
of movement and the chord line, the lift and
drag of the aerofoil will change in magnitude.

00:06:11.659 --> 00:06:16.229
We can map this with the lift drag ratio,
which is essentially our efficiency curve

00:06:16.229 --> 00:06:21.370
for our aerofoil, we can see that the wing
generates the most lift, for the least drag

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:25.409
penalty at an angle of attack of around 2-4
degrees.[3]

00:06:25.409 --> 00:06:30.979
A propellor is essentially an aerofoil, but
it’s free body diagram is a little more

00:06:30.979 --> 00:06:36.629
complicated, because not only is the propellor
aerofoil moving forward relative to the plane's

00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:42.300
direction of movement, but it is also rotating
around the axis of the drive shaft.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:45.199
Let’s name these two separate velocities.

00:06:45.199 --> 00:06:49.879
Propeller velocity is the speed of the propeller
rotation, and plane velocity (aeroplane) will

00:06:49.879 --> 00:06:52.350
be the forward velocity of the plane.

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:56.159
These two velocities combine into a new vector
called the blade path.

00:06:56.159 --> 00:07:02.309
[4] This blade path angle is not at the same
angle as the angle of attack of the propellor

00:07:02.309 --> 00:07:08.400
blade, but critically, the blade path is the
ACTUAL angle of attack of air travelling over

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:09.539
the propellor.

00:07:09.539 --> 00:07:13.809
To understand this, let’s see what happens
to a fixed pitch propeller when we increase

00:07:13.809 --> 00:07:15.759
our propeller velocity.

00:07:15.759 --> 00:07:22.089
Right now, we are at the optimum angle of
attack of around 3 degrees, but if we increase

00:07:22.089 --> 00:07:27.600
the propeller velocity the angle of attack
begins to increase, reducing the propeller

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.400
efficiency, the same happens if we lower the
propeller velocity.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:35.860
Both move us away from the optimum angle of
attack.

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:41.509
We can set the rotation speed, but remember
when we said that the propeller velocity changes

00:07:41.509 --> 00:07:46.900
along the length of the propeller, with the
tip having much higher speeds than the root?

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:52.930
That means if the propellor was just one fixed
angle of attack only one section of the propellor

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:55.479
would be at the optimum angle of attack.

00:07:55.479 --> 00:08:01.979
So, the propeller blade twists along its length
to ensure each part is working efficiently,

00:08:01.979 --> 00:08:07.639
but the forward movement of the plane also
affects this angle of attack.

00:08:07.639 --> 00:08:12.431
For early planes, who’s top speed did not
get that high, having a fixed pitch propeller

00:08:12.431 --> 00:08:14.940
didn’t matter that much.

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:20.610
The forward movement of the plane only marginally
affected the angle of attack on the propeller,

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:26.389
but as speeds increased variable pitch propellers
began to become commonplace.

00:08:26.389 --> 00:08:31.479
Allowing the propellers to rotate and adjust
to higher speeds and maintain the optimum

00:08:31.479 --> 00:08:32.639
angle.

00:08:32.639 --> 00:08:37.219
This was essential for the Thunderscreech,
and you can see it has a very large range

00:08:37.219 --> 00:08:42.740
of rotation for it’s short propellers, to
ensure it can operate at the large range of

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:45.600
speeds it was expected to encounter.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:50.480
The blade profile of supersonic aerofoils
are different from normal propellers too.

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:54.759
Research into this began in World War 2, as
the engines were getting continually more

00:08:54.759 --> 00:09:00.509
powerful the prospect of propellers operating
in transonic and supersonic ranges was becoming

00:09:00.509 --> 00:09:02.339
more and more likely.

00:09:02.339 --> 00:09:07.240
With little to no empirical data available
researchers were forced to build specialized

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:12.480
wind tunnels and measurement technologies
to research how different propellers would

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:13.480
operate.

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:18.480
They tested swept back tips, after data obtained
from Germany showed its merit, but manufacturing

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:22.650
difficulties and marginal improvements discredited
the idea.

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:27.740
The engineers, after much trial and error,
landed on making the propeller blades as thin

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:28.740
as possible.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:34.040
Tapering from a thickness ratio of 5% at the
base to just 2% at the tip.

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:38.360
[5] That is the ratio of the aerofoil depth,
divided by its width.

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:43.839
The propeller was also to have sharp leading
edges with little to no chamber.

00:09:43.839 --> 00:09:49.170
When tested, these propellers were capable
of maintaining their efficiency much higher

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:51.830
and over a wider range of speeds.

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:57.470
However, even with the propellor efficiency
improved, the drag associated with the formation

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:03.550
of shock waves over the entire propeller demanded
an obscenely powerful engine.

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:10.410
The XF-84 had an engine horsepower of a whopping
5850hp, nearly 4 times more powerful than

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:12.390
a typical Supermarine Spitfire.

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:16.440
[6] The engine installed in the Thunderscreech
was unusual.

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:21.949
The engine was actually two engines connected
together to power a common drive shaft.

00:10:21.949 --> 00:10:23.519
This is called a coupled engine.

00:10:23.519 --> 00:10:29.680
If a suitably powerful engine is not available,
it’s often easier to just couple two engines

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:34.519
to reach the total power needed, rather than
developing a completely new engine.

00:10:34.519 --> 00:10:36.949
The practice does have some other advantages.

00:10:36.949 --> 00:10:41.100
[7]
The coupled T-40-A-1 engines of the Thunderscreech

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:43.470
were placed inside the fuselage.

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:48.111
Typically uncoupled engines are wing mounted,
which creates a lot of additional drag, which

00:10:48.111 --> 00:10:52.019
is avoided when the engines are placed with
the fuselage.

00:10:52.019 --> 00:10:57.360
Wing mounted engines also come with some control
issues in the event of an engine failure.

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:02.470
If one engine stops working our working engine
creates asymmetric thrust, which will cause

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:05.960
the plane to yaw in the direction of the faulty
engine.

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:11.529
With two coupled engines driving a single
propeller our thrust remains on the centreline,

00:11:11.529 --> 00:11:13.600
just at half the power.

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:17.640
Finally, coupled engines had some fuel consumption
advantages.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:23.620
Turbine engines typically reach their highest
efficiency at higher power settings, but military

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:28.259
planes often operate well below their highest
thrust setting.

00:11:28.259 --> 00:11:33.660
This graph plots the specific fuel consumption
vs horsepower for a single engine.

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:39.630
We can see that at 3400 horsepower the engine
has a lower specific fuel consumption than

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:45.209
at half power of 1700, meaning it is operating
more efficiently.

00:11:45.209 --> 00:11:50.860
One of the advantages of coupled engines is
that each engine can run the propeller independently,

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:56.060
so if we wanted to run at half power, we could
just turn off one of the engines and run one

00:11:56.060 --> 00:12:00.040
of the coupled engines at the more efficient
peak power of 3400.

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:05.250
Whereas, if we have two separate engines mounted
on the wings, because of that asymmetric thrust

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:10.329
issue, we would have to run each at the less
efficient half power setting.

00:12:10.329 --> 00:12:14.029
Increasing fuel consumption and decreasing
range and loiter time.

00:12:14.029 --> 00:12:18.440
The powerful coupled engines did come with
some added complexity that caused a lot of

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:19.440
issues.

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:24.690
The engine was mounted behind the pilot, with
air intakes placed in the root of the wings.

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:28.959
This wouldn’t be weird for a modern day
jet engine which derives its thrust from a

00:12:28.959 --> 00:12:31.459
nozzle at the rear of the aircraft, but this
is a prop plane.

00:12:31.459 --> 00:12:36.089
The Thunderscreech had to somehow transfer
power from the back of the plane to the front,

00:12:36.089 --> 00:12:40.680
and it did this with two drive shafts running
on either side of the pilot.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:46.779
Which is a bit terrifying considering these
driveshafts were turning 14,300 revolutions

00:12:46.779 --> 00:12:50.589
per minute, and caused the plane to violently
vibrate.

00:12:50.589 --> 00:12:55.990
This RPM had to be reduced before reaching
the propeller, so the driveshafts fed into

00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:59.550
a 6.8:1 two stage reduction gear.

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:05.040
To bring the propeller RPM down to 2100 revolutions
per minute.

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:09.470
Much slower, but that speed still caused some
serious issues for control.

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:14.290
When the propeller spins clockwise, it creates
an opposite reaction force that could cause

00:13:14.290 --> 00:13:17.100
the actual plane body to spin anticlockwise.

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:20.339
[8]
With the extremely high rotation speeds of

00:13:20.339 --> 00:13:26.201
this propeller this effect was particularly
pronounced, and became even worse as the plane

00:13:26.201 --> 00:13:27.670
picked up speed.

00:13:27.670 --> 00:13:32.000
According to Hank Beaird, a test pilot who
flew 12 flights with the Thunderscreech:

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:36.600
It became a handful…when you got it out
around 400 knots,” “The propeller governor

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:39.519
would start surging, and the airplane would
roll rather violently.”

00:13:39.519 --> 00:13:43.699
“The entire airplane, in fact, was trying
to torque itself around the propshaft, rotating

00:13:43.699 --> 00:13:46.511
left while the prop spun to the right.”

00:13:46.511 --> 00:13:48.250
[9]
The propeller governor is the mechanical device

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:53.310
designed to sense the speed of the propeller,
and control its pitch.

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:59.389
At a particular speed it seems that the sheer
inertia of the heavy fast spinning propeller

00:13:59.389 --> 00:14:04.009
overloaded the control system and caused it
to malfunction, causing the propeller to increase

00:14:04.009 --> 00:14:08.930
in speed and cause even more torquing motion
of the aircraft.

00:14:08.930 --> 00:14:14.339
This torque effect was present at all speeds
of course, and the designers of the Thunderscreech

00:14:14.339 --> 00:14:16.420
were well aware of the problem.

00:14:16.420 --> 00:14:22.481
This affected many propeller fighters of WW2,
it affected the rotary engines of WW1 even

00:14:22.481 --> 00:14:23.740
more.

00:14:23.740 --> 00:14:29.839
Rotary engines rotated around a fixed crankshaft,
meaning the entire engine rotated with the

00:14:29.839 --> 00:14:33.819
propellor, which helped tremendously with
cooling and helped bring down the weight of

00:14:33.819 --> 00:14:40.310
the engine for these underpowered aircraft,
but the gyroscopic precession was so strong

00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:46.600
that the Sopwith Camel had to use left rudder
for both left AND right turns.

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:51.550
The torque effect is usually most prominent
at lower speeds where the air travelling over

00:14:51.550 --> 00:14:55.580
the plane has less power to resist the rolling
motion.

00:14:55.580 --> 00:15:00.910
Without counter action the torque could roll
the Thunderscreech up to 30 degrees off centre

00:15:00.910 --> 00:15:01.910
line.

00:15:01.910 --> 00:15:07.790
Using the ailerons to counteract roll is possible,
but that introduces adverse yaw, where the

00:15:07.790 --> 00:15:13.930
increased drag caused by the actuated aileron
causes the plane to yaw, so the designers

00:15:13.930 --> 00:15:19.170
of the Thunderscreech included this anti-torque
vane just behind the cockpit.

00:15:19.170 --> 00:15:25.089
On take off and landings the vane was deflected
45 degrees, which caused a counteracting lift

00:15:25.089 --> 00:15:28.540
that acted around the rolling axis of the
plane.

00:15:28.540 --> 00:15:33.470
Another unique design feature of the Thunderscreech
is it’s T-tail configuration, which particularly

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:40.149
stands out because it required a modification
from the F-84F Thunderstreak jet fighter which

00:15:40.149 --> 00:15:42.380
the Thunderscreech was based on.

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:47.899
This was done to get the stabiliser out of
the way of prop wash and the turbulence associated

00:15:47.899 --> 00:15:52.690
with it, which could reduce authority of the
stabiliser during pitch.

00:15:52.690 --> 00:15:56.089
The plane was still a serious handful to handle.

00:15:56.089 --> 00:16:01.100
Leading to one hilarious quote from Lin Hendricks
to Jim Rust, Republic’s chief engineer

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:05.540
“You aren’t big enough and there aren’t
enough of you to get me in that thing again.”

00:16:05.540 --> 00:16:11.399
Test pilots are a unique breed of crazy, yet
this one knew this plane was not safe to fly.

00:16:11.399 --> 00:16:16.720
10 out of eleven flights for Hank Beaird ended
in an emergency landing.

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:22.790
The engine was so unreliable the plane flew
with it’s ram air turbine constantly deployed

00:16:22.790 --> 00:16:24.529
just as a precaution.

00:16:24.529 --> 00:16:30.579
The turbine could use ram air to power it’s
electronics if the engines failed.

00:16:30.579 --> 00:16:35.540
Between this, the plane violently vibrating
from the two drive shafts spinning at 14,000

00:16:35.540 --> 00:16:41.720
RPM on both sides of the pilot, the sudden
rolling due to propeller surges, and ofcourse

00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:47.199
the immense sound that was capable of knocking
people over, the plane never got past the

00:16:47.199 --> 00:16:48.730
testing phase.

00:16:48.730 --> 00:16:51.439
The program was soon cancelled

00:16:51.439 --> 00:16:57.779
Weird experimental projects like this are
often the only way we can learn new things.

00:16:57.779 --> 00:17:01.190
Just trying something way outside of what
we already know.

00:17:01.190 --> 00:17:07.799
That was our goal when we started our Nebula
